WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.661
A small company's app records card payments, and it lists its 13 customers, noting who invited each one.

2
00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:14.705
Asha's two friends got a free coffee when they joined with her invitation.

3
00:00:14.705 --> 00:00:20.315
Now the company wants to send one to everybody else: every customer Asha didn't invite.

4
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.346
That should be 11 people.

5
00:00:22.346 --> 00:00:26.746
You ask the database, and it answers at once, with no error: 6.

6
00:00:26.746 --> 00:00:28.492
Where did the other 5 go?

7
00:00:28.833 --> 00:00:30.212
Look at the column.

8
00:00:30.212 --> 00:00:34.710
referred_by holds the id of the customer who invited this one.

9
00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:38.048
Asha is customer 1, so Chloe and Elena hold 1.

10
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:42.048
But Kofi, customer 11, signed up without an invitation.

11
00:00:42.048 --> 00:00:44.277
What should his referred_by hold?

12
00:00:44.567 --> 00:00:45.008
Try 0.

13
00:00:45.008 --> 00:00:52.216
There's no customer 0, and the table's rule that referred_by must name a real customer rejects it.

14
00:00:52.216 --> 00:00:55.893
And a count of invited customers would count Kofi.

15
00:00:56.133 --> 00:01:01.782
Try -1: the same problems, and every query must remember what -1 means.

16
00:01:01.782 --> 00:01:06.237
SQL's answer is a marker that means no value here: NULL.

17
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.998
Picture NULL as a sealed envelope.

18
00:01:08.998 --> 00:01:12.360
There may be a number inside, but nobody can see it.

19
00:01:12.633 --> 00:01:14.632
Is the envelope equal to 1?

20
00:01:14.632 --> 00:01:17.075
The honest answer is: can't tell.

21
00:01:17.075 --> 00:01:18.925
And that's what SQL says.

22
00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:22.681
Ask whether NULL equals 1, and SQL doesn't say false.

23
00:01:22.681 --> 00:01:25.665
It says NULL again, which here means unknown.

24
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.697
Honestly, NULL should have been called don't know.

25
00:01:28.697 --> 00:01:31.439
Every rule makes sense once you read it that way.

26
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.191
So SQL has three answers: true, false and unknown.

27
00:01:35.191 --> 00:01:37.495
That's called three-valued logic.

28
00:01:37.867 --> 00:01:38.840
Now the query.

29
00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.482
WHERE runs its test once for each row.

30
00:01:41.482 --> 00:01:44.610
Does a row with a sealed envelope stay or go?

31
00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:45.723
Pause and guess.

32
00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:53.057
Asha, customer 1: NULL not equal to 1 is unknown.

33
00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:58.167
And here's the rule that bites: WHERE keeps a row only when its test is true.

34
00:01:58.167 --> 00:02:00.759
False drops it, and unknown drops it too.

35
00:02:01.033 --> 00:02:04.924
So Asha goes, though she certainly didn't invite herself.

36
00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:08.815
Chloe: 1 not equal to 1 is false, so she goes, correctly.

37
00:02:08.815 --> 00:02:11.955
Daniel: 2 not equal to 1 is true, so he stays.

38
00:02:12.233 --> 00:02:14.323
And so on, down the table.

39
00:02:14.323 --> 00:02:19.226
Five envelopes, five rows gone, with no error and no warning.

40
00:02:19.226 --> 00:02:19.869
6 of 13.

41
00:02:20.233 --> 00:02:22.774
A few rules follow from the envelope.

42
00:02:22.774 --> 00:02:30.807
Any comparison with NULL is unknown, even NULL equals NULL: two sealed envelopes may or may not hold the same number.

43
00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:35.070
IS NULL asks a different question: is this a sealed envelope?

44
00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:36.776
That always has an answer.

45
00:02:37.033 --> 00:02:43.004
When tests are joined with OR, or with AND, a side we already know can decide on its own.

46
00:02:43.004 --> 00:02:46.560
true OR unknown is true, whatever the envelope holds.

47
00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.371
false AND unknown is false.

48
00:02:48.371 --> 00:02:50.585
And NOT unknown is still unknown.

49
00:02:50.933 --> 00:02:52.433
So say what you mean.

50
00:02:52.433 --> 00:02:57.005
referred_by IS DISTINCT FROM 1 treats NULL as an ordinary value.

51
00:02:57.267 --> 00:03:05.465
An envelope is distinct from 1, so the test is true, and the five come back: 11 rows, everyone Asha didn't invite.

52
00:03:05.733 --> 00:03:09.707
Writing not equal to 1, OR referred_by IS NULL, does the same.

53
00:03:09.707 --> 00:03:15.603
And if a column should never be empty, declare it NOT NULL, and the question never comes up.

54
00:03:15.967 --> 00:03:16.848
Two warnings.

55
00:03:16.848 --> 00:03:22.745
If you write Kotlin or Java, this feels wrong, because there, null equals null is true.

56
00:03:22.745 --> 00:03:26.269
SQL spells that kind of equal: IS NOT DISTINCT FROM.

57
00:03:26.533 --> 00:03:34.881
And the envelope picture breaks in one place: when SQL groups or sorts rows, it puts all the NULLs together, as if they were equal.

58
00:03:35.133 --> 00:03:37.184
Counting has its own rules.

59
00:03:37.184 --> 00:03:39.083
count(*) counts rows: 13.

60
00:03:39.083 --> 00:03:42.881
count(referred_by) counts the filled-in values: 8.

61
00:03:42.881 --> 00:03:46.147
And sum over no rows at all is NULL, not 0.

62
00:03:46.467 --> 00:03:52.587
So: NULL is a sealed envelope, any comparison with it is unknown, and WHERE keeps only true.

63
00:03:52.587 --> 00:03:56.379
Next time a filter says not, ask which side can be empty.

64
00:03:56.733 --> 00:03:57.912
One quick check.

65
00:03:57.912 --> 00:04:01.447
count(*) says 13, and count(referred_by) says 8.

66
00:04:01.447 --> 00:04:05.203
How many customers would a careless not equal lose?

67
00:04:05.203 --> 00:04:06.381
Pause and think.

68
00:04:09.567 --> 00:04:09.699
5.

69
00:04:09.699 --> 00:04:15.937
The gap between the counts is the number of sealed envelopes, and every one of them falls out.
